@database "request_logger"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: request_logger.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Request Logger"
@{h1}Request Logger

Writes a trace of what the agents do with the LLM into the server's normal
log: a line before and after every LLM call of an agent run, with the
session, the agent, how long the call took and the first characters of the
last message and of the answer; a line when a session starts; and a
summary line when a run ends. It is meant for watching the flow while
debugging, and doubles as the simplest example of a hook plugin. It has no
tools and no panel, changes nothing the model sees, and is off in the
default configuration.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
  @{"  What gets logged  " link log}
  @{"  Privacy, access and limits  " link privacy}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The four hooks  " link hooks}
@{body}
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The entry in @{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt} is there, switched off:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    request_logger:
      type: request_logger
      enabled: false
@{body}

Set @{tt}enabled: true@{utt} and restart the server. No allowlist line is
needed -- the plugin has no tools -- and no extra packages. Once it is on,
its hooks run for @{b}every@{ub} agent and sub-agent, the chat included;
see @{" The four hooks " link hooks} for switching them off per agent.

@{h2}Settings

The settings go in a @{tt}config:@{utt} block of the server entry -- keys
written directly in the entry are not read:

@{code yaml}
    request_logger:
      type: request_logger
      enabled: true
      config:
        log_level: DEBUG
        log_message_content: false
@{body}

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
log_level | INFO | the level of every line the plugin writes: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING or ERROR; anything else is INFO
log_message_content | true | false: no text from the conversation, only counts, IDs and times
log_timing | true | false: no durations
max_content_preview | 100 | characters of a message or answer shown; held to 10 ... 1000
@{body}

They are read once, when the plugin starts. An agent's hook override cannot
change them.

@{h2}Where the lines go

The plugin opens no file of its own. It writes through the process's
logger @{tt}plugins.request_logger.hooks@{utt}, so the lines land where the
@{tt}logging:@{utt} block of @{tt}config/config.yaml@{utt} sends them: the
API into @{tt}logs/api.log@{utt}, @{tt}agent-cli@{utt} into
@{tt}logs/cli.log@{utt}, and the console. A line is written only when its
level is at or above the level configured there; with the shipped
@{tt}level: DEBUG@{utt} everything is written, with @{tt}level: INFO@{utt} a
@{tt}log_level: DEBUG@{utt} here silences the plugin in the files.
@endnode

@node log "What gets logged"
@{h1}What gets logged

One run of an agent with two LLM calls, at the default settings:

@{code}
[RequestLogger] Session Started: session_id=abc123, agent=chat_agent
[RequestLogger] Request #1 - Pre-LLM Call: session=abc123, messages=3, agent=chat_agent
[RequestLogger] Last message: role=user, content=What is in the report folder?
[RequestLogger] Request #1 - Post-LLM Call: duration=2345.67ms, response_preview=
[RequestLogger] Request #2 - Pre-LLM Call: session=abc123, messages=5, agent=chat_agent
[RequestLogger] Last message: role=tool, content={"files": ["q3.pdf", "notes.txt"]}
[RequestLogger] Request #2 - Post-LLM Call: duration=1234.56ms, response_preview=There are two files...
[RequestLogger] Run Ended: session_id=abc123, llm_calls=2, duration=3.61s
@{body}

@{bullet}@{b}Request #@{ub} counts the LLM calls of the whole process, across
all sessions, from 1 at start; the post line repeats the number of its call.
@{bullet}@{b}messages@{ub} is the length of the history the call is about to
send, system prompt included.
@{bullet}@{b}Last message@{ub} is the last message of that history -- the
user's text, or a tool result. Text that is not plain (images) is shown as
its Python form.
@{bullet}@{b}duration@{ub} on the post line is the time from this plugin's
pre hook to its post hook: the LLM call with its retries and fallbacks,
plus the other hooks that run in between (a context compaction can take
seconds).
@{bullet}@{b}response_preview@{ub} is the answer's text. An answer that only
calls tools has none, so it stays empty; tool calls and their arguments are
not logged.
@{bullet}@{b}Session Started@{ub} is written once, when a session without
history gets its first run; a session resumed with its history has none.
@{bullet}@{b}Run Ended@{ub} is written at the end of every run (every
message the user sends), with that run's LLM calls and the time from its
first LLM call.

Plugins that call an LLM on their own, outside an agent's steps, are not
seen.
@endnode

@node privacy "Privacy, access and limits"
@{h1}Privacy, access and limits

@{h2}Secrets

The plugin sees the conversation, not the HTTP traffic: no headers, no
Authorization, no cookies, no URLs of the provider. What it can write is
text from the conversation -- up to @{tt}max_content_preview@{utt}
characters of the last message and of the answer. That text is not
masked. A key the user pasted into the chat, or a tool result that shows a
config file, reaches the log as far as it falls within the preview. The
only masking on the way is the log handlers' own: they hide the key of a
callback URL (@{tt}/plugins/<name>/callback/***@{utt}) in every line.
Set @{tt}log_message_content: false@{utt} to keep conversation text out
entirely.

@{h2}Who can read it

The lines are part of the server's shared log files, not stored per user:
every user's prompts and answers land in the same file. Who can read them:

@{bullet}admins, in the Logs panel (its endpoints need the admin role);
@{bullet}any agent whose allowlist has the @{tt}log_viewer@{utt} tools, for
the files configured there -- whoever talks to that agent;
@{bullet}anyone with file access to @{tt}logs/@{utt} on the server.

On a server with several users, switch the content off or leave the plugin
off.

@{h2}Size and rotation

The plugin adds up to three lines per LLM call. The files rotate as the
@{tt}logging:@{utt} block says -- shipped: at 10 MB, keeping 5 old files
(@{tt}api.log.1@{utt} ... @{tt}api.log.5@{utt}); the oldest is deleted. With
rotation switched off there, the file is started fresh at each start of
the process and grows without bound.

@{h2}When writing fails

A write that fails (disk full, file gone) is handled by Python's logging:
the line is lost, a traceback goes to the console, and the request goes on.
A log file locked for rotation by another process makes the write wait for
the lock, as every other log line of the process does. An error inside a
hook is logged and the call goes on; the pre and post hooks have 2 seconds,
the session hooks 1.

@{h2}Cost

Each enabled hook gets its own copy of the conversation from the hook
system, so the plugin adds two deep copies of the history per LLM call --
noticeable only on very long conversations. It sends nothing to the model
and adds no tokens.
@endnode

@node hooks "The four hooks"
@{h1}The four hooks

@{table}
Hook | Type | When
log_pre_llm | pre_llm_call | before every LLM call of an agent step
log_post_llm | post_llm_call | after it, once the answer is complete
log_session_start | session_start | the first run of a session without history
log_session_end | session_end | the end of every run, after the conversation is saved
@{body}

All four only write log lines: they change neither the messages nor the
answer, so they do not disturb the prompt cache and other hooks see the
same context as without them. They run for every agent that has hooks on
(the default), sub-agents and the chat included. What the pre hook
measures is kept in the plugin per run (session and request), and dropped
when the run ends; at most 1000 runs are kept at once, the oldest dropped
first.

@{h2}Switching them off

For one agent, in its YAML -- one hook by its full name:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      request_logger.log_pre_llm:
        enabled: false
@{body}

For all agents, the same under @{tt}hooks: overrides:@{utt} at the end of
@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}; there the key @{tt}request_logger@{utt}
alone covers all four hooks. @{tt}hook_config: {enabled: false}@{utt} in the
server entry registers them switched off. An agent's override wins over
both.

With @{tt}log_pre_llm@{utt} off, the post line has neither the call number
(@{tt}#?@{utt}) nor a duration, and the run summary counts no calls.
@endnode
